WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.559
A doubly linked list node has three parts

00:00:03.559 --> 00:00:04.568
the element

00:00:04.568 --> 00:00:07.594
a reference prev to the previous node

00:00:07.594 --> 00:00:10.620
and a reference next to the next node.

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:14.212
Here is the list 13, 15

00:00:14.212 --> 00:00:16.343
19, 57.

00:00:16.343 --> 00:00:19.399
Every arrow now comes in a pair.

00:00:19.399 --> 00:00:22.271
The first node's prev is null

00:00:22.271 --> 00:00:25.421
and the last node's next is null.

00:00:25.421 --> 00:00:29.589
We usually keep both a head, the first node

00:00:29.589 --> 00:00:32.090
and a tail, the last node.

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:35.753
In memory, as in the slides' table

00:00:35.753 --> 00:00:38.396
each node is a row: the data

00:00:38.396 --> 00:00:41.391
the address of the previous node

00:00:41.391 --> 00:00:43.946
and the address of the next

00:00:43.946 --> 00:00:46.941
with -1 standing for null.

00:00:46.941 --> 00:00:50.288
Head is 1: node 1 holds 13

00:00:50.288 --> 00:00:53.371
with prev -1 and next 4.

00:00:53.371 --> 00:00:55.574
Node 4 holds 15

00:00:55.574 --> 00:00:59.009
with prev 1 and next 6, and so on

00:00:59.009 --> 00:01:01.740
up to node 8, 57

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:03.590
with next -1.

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:08.142
Following next from node 1 gives 13

00:01:08.142 --> 00:01:11.198
15, 19, 57.

00:01:11.294 --> 00:01:14.159
Following prev from node 8

00:01:14.254 --> 00:01:16.260
gives them backwards.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:18.864
What the extra link buys

00:01:18.864 --> 00:01:21.698
backward traversal, from the tail

00:01:21.698 --> 00:01:22.993
following prev.

00:01:22.993 --> 00:01:25.746
Deleting a node you already hold

00:01:25.746 --> 00:01:29.389
in O(1): the node knows its predecessor

00:01:29.389 --> 00:01:31.332
so no search is needed.

00:01:31.413 --> 00:01:34.004
In a singly linked list you must

00:01:34.085 --> 00:01:36.514
walk from the head to find it.

00:01:36.514 --> 00:01:38.700
And with a tail reference

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:41.614
deleting the last node is O(1)

00:01:41.614 --> 00:01:43.800
tail = tail.prev.

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:47.352
What it costs: one more reference per node

00:01:47.427 --> 00:01:50.033
and every insertion or deletion has

00:01:50.108 --> 00:01:53.310
up to four links to update, instead of two.

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:56.280
Insert after a node at.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:00.955
Here at is 15, and we insert 17.

00:02:00.955 --> 00:02:02.930
Make the new node.

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:05.631
Step 1: its prev is at.

00:02:05.631 --> 00:02:09.891
Step 2: its next is the node after at

00:02:09.891 --> 00:02:10.930
19.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.709
If at was the last node

00:02:13.709 --> 00:02:16.847
the new node becomes the tail.

00:02:16.847 --> 00:02:19.175
Otherwise, step 3

00:02:19.276 --> 00:02:22.819
the old successor's prev now points

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.045
back to the new node.

00:02:25.045 --> 00:02:29.600
Step four: at.next points to the new node.

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:32.683
Four links, all in O(1).

00:02:32.683 --> 00:02:36.684
In the demo, this gives 8, 13

00:02:36.684 --> 00:02:40.880
15, 17, 19, 57

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:43.710
after insertFirst(8).

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:45.591
Now predict.

00:02:45.591 --> 00:02:50.143
Could step four, at.next = node

00:02:50.143 --> 00:02:53.069
be done before step three

00:02:53.069 --> 00:02:56.755
at.next.prev = node?

00:02:56.755 --> 00:03:01.090
Pause the video, and follow the arrows.

00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:04.926
No.

00:03:04.926 --> 00:03:08.346
Go back to before steps three and four.

00:03:08.346 --> 00:03:12.021
If at.next becomes the new node first

00:03:12.106 --> 00:03:14.500
then at.next.prev is

00:03:14.585 --> 00:03:16.637
the new node's own prev

00:03:16.637 --> 00:03:20.056
the new node now points back to itself.

00:03:20.056 --> 00:03:23.902
And 19's prev still points at 15

00:03:23.902 --> 00:03:27.150
so walking backwards skips 17.

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:28.333
The rule

00:03:28.426 --> 00:03:31.665
update the far neighbour before you

00:03:31.757 --> 00:03:34.811
change the link that leads to it.

00:03:34.811 --> 00:03:37.125
Done in the right order

00:03:37.125 --> 00:03:41.012
the list is 13, 15, 17

00:03:41.012 --> 00:03:43.140
19, 57.

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:47.592
Remove a node, here 19, in O(1)

00:03:47.592 --> 00:03:50.358
its neighbours point past it.

00:03:50.358 --> 00:03:53.585
The previous node's next skips it

00:03:53.585 --> 00:03:57.734
and the next node's prev skips it backwards.

00:03:57.734 --> 00:03:59.117
At either end

00:03:59.117 --> 00:04:02.160
head or tail is updated instead.

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:05.412
No search for the predecessor is needed.

00:04:05.412 --> 00:04:08.023
Printing backwards is a good check

00:04:08.023 --> 00:04:11.070
a forgotten prev update shows up at once.

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:14.569
The demo prints the list forwards and

00:04:14.648 --> 00:04:16.937
backwards after every change.

00:04:17.015 --> 00:04:20.014
After insertFirst(8) and

00:04:20.093 --> 00:04:23.564
insertAfter(15, 17): 8, 13

00:04:23.564 --> 00:04:26.957
15, 17, 19, 57.

00:04:26.957 --> 00:04:28.930
After removing 19

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:31.770
forwards and backwards still agree.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:34.407
deleteFirst returns 8

00:04:34.407 --> 00:04:37.541
and deleteLast returns 57

00:04:37.541 --> 00:04:39.066
both in O(1).

00:04:39.066 --> 00:04:42.200
13, 15, 17 remain.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:45.323
Doubly linked lists are everywhere

00:04:45.397 --> 00:04:47.192
you go back and forward

00:04:47.192 --> 00:04:50.034
a browser's history, an image viewer

00:04:50.108 --> 00:04:52.053
the previous and next song

00:04:52.127 --> 00:04:53.473
in a music player.

00:04:53.473 --> 00:04:56.240
They build other structures: deques

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:57.138
LRU caches

00:04:57.138 --> 00:04:59.680
and Java's own linked list class

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:01.475
which is doubly linked.

00:05:01.549 --> 00:05:04.615
And an operating system's scheduler keeps

00:05:04.690 --> 00:05:07.158
processes in doubly linked lists

00:05:07.233 --> 00:05:10.299
so it can unlink a process from one queue

00:05:10.373 --> 00:05:13.140
and link it into another in O(1).

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:17.653
A circular linked list is a singly linked list

00:05:17.739 --> 00:05:21.159
whose last node links back to the first

00:05:21.159 --> 00:05:22.869
instead of to null.

00:05:22.955 --> 00:05:25.691
The trick is to keep a reference

00:05:25.777 --> 00:05:27.230
to the last node.

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:29.710
A list of one node, 15

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:31.163
links to itself.

00:05:31.249 --> 00:05:33.729
insertLast(19) first

00:05:33.814 --> 00:05:35.866
inserts it at the front

00:05:35.866 --> 00:05:39.116
the new node points to the old first

00:05:39.116 --> 00:05:41.937
and last points to the new node.

00:05:41.937 --> 00:05:44.417
Then last moves on one node

00:05:44.417 --> 00:05:46.640
and 19 is the last.

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:49.070
insertLast(57)

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:50.670
works the same way.

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:52.750
insertFirst(13)

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:55.790
the new node points to the old first

00:05:55.790 --> 00:05:59.070
15, and the last node, 57

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:00.670
points to 13.

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:04.427
So the first node is always last.next

00:06:04.427 --> 00:06:06.779
both ends are one step away.

00:06:06.779 --> 00:06:08.482
Insert at the front,

00:06:08.563 --> 00:06:11.078
insert at the end, and delete at

00:06:11.159 --> 00:06:13.349
the front are all O(1)

00:06:13.349 --> 00:06:15.620
exactly what a queue needs.

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:18.898
There is no null in a circular list

00:06:18.973 --> 00:06:21.520
so a traversal stops when it comes

00:06:21.595 --> 00:06:23.469
back to where it started.

00:06:23.469 --> 00:06:26.541
The test, are we back at the first node,

00:06:26.541 --> 00:06:28.564
is true at the very start

00:06:28.564 --> 00:06:30.587
so the loop is a do while

00:06:30.587 --> 00:06:32.985
it checks after the first step.

00:06:32.985 --> 00:06:36.357
The demo prints 13, 15, 19,

00:06:36.357 --> 00:06:38.680
57, back to 13.

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:41.276
deleteFirst makes the last

00:06:41.359 --> 00:06:44.936
node skip the first one: 15, 19,

00:06:44.936 --> 00:06:47.432
57, back to 15.

00:06:47.515 --> 00:06:50.260
But delete at the end still needs

00:06:50.343 --> 00:06:53.670
the predecessor of last: a walk, O(n).

00:06:54.250 --> 00:06:56.820
Circular lists fit round-robin

00:06:56.906 --> 00:07:00.332
CPU scheduling: every ready process gets

00:07:00.418 --> 00:07:04.016
the same time slice, the quantum, in turn

00:07:04.016 --> 00:07:07.100
round and round, until it finishes.

00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:09.242
Here the quantum is 2

00:07:09.242 --> 00:07:12.411
and four processes need 5, 3

00:07:12.411 --> 00:07:14.810
1 and 4 units of time.

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:18.003
P1 runs from 0 to 2

00:07:18.003 --> 00:07:20.643
and has 3 units left.

00:07:20.643 --> 00:07:23.689
P2 runs to 4, 1 left.

00:07:23.689 --> 00:07:28.056
P3 needs only 1 unit: done at 5

00:07:28.056 --> 00:07:31.711
and it is unlinked from the circle.

00:07:31.711 --> 00:07:34.960
P4 runs to 7, 2 left.

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:38.675
Round again: P1 runs to 9.

00:07:38.675 --> 00:07:41.093
P2 finishes at 10.

00:07:41.093 --> 00:07:43.932
P4 finishes at 12

00:07:43.932 --> 00:07:46.350
and P1 at 13.

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:48.841
To remove a finished process

00:07:48.917 --> 00:07:50.140
from the circle

00:07:50.140 --> 00:07:52.740
the loop needs the one before it.

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:55.492
So it carries prev along with curr

00:07:55.568 --> 00:07:57.938
in a singly linked circle there

00:07:58.015 --> 00:07:59.620
is no other way back.

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:03.742
Doubly linked or circular: which is better?

00:08:03.742 --> 00:08:07.201
It is a trade-off between time and space.

00:08:07.283 --> 00:08:09.425
A doubly linked list makes

00:08:09.507 --> 00:08:11.154
deleting at the end

00:08:11.154 --> 00:08:14.367
or deleting a node you hold, O(1)

00:08:14.367 --> 00:08:16.590
and lets you go backwards.

00:08:16.590 --> 00:08:19.720
It pays 1 extra reference per node.

00:08:20.070 --> 00:08:22.746
A circular list costs no extra space

00:08:22.819 --> 00:08:24.627
and makes both ends cheap

00:08:24.699 --> 00:08:26.435
with a single reference

00:08:26.507 --> 00:08:28.894
all that a queue or a round-robin

00:08:28.967 --> 00:08:30.124
scheduler needs.

00:08:30.124 --> 00:08:32.004
Neither speeds up search

00:08:32.077 --> 00:08:34.391
finding a key is O(n) in every

00:08:34.464 --> 00:08:35.910
kind of linked list.

00:08:36.490 --> 00:08:39.450
A doubly circular list combines both

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:41.850
every node has prev and next

00:08:41.850 --> 00:08:44.650
the last node.next is the first

00:08:44.650 --> 00:08:47.770
and the first node.prev is the last.

00:08:47.770 --> 00:08:51.050
A clean way to build it uses a sentinel

00:08:51.050 --> 00:08:53.290
a dummy node in the circle

00:08:53.290 --> 00:08:55.370
called L.nil in CLRS.

00:08:55.370 --> 00:08:58.730
In an empty list, the sentinel.prev and

00:08:58.810 --> 00:09:00.490
next point to itself.

00:09:00.490 --> 00:09:04.090
linkAfter puts a new node after any node a

00:09:04.090 --> 00:09:05.450
with 4 links.

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:09.197
insertLast(2), insertLast(3)

00:09:09.197 --> 00:09:10.937
insertFirst(1).

00:09:10.937 --> 00:09:14.002
sentinel.next is the first node

00:09:14.002 --> 00:09:16.901
sentinel.prev is the last.

00:09:16.984 --> 00:09:19.138
Every real node always has

00:09:19.221 --> 00:09:20.961
a real prev and next

00:09:21.044 --> 00:09:23.529
so insertion and deletion have

00:09:23.612 --> 00:09:25.600
no special cases at all.

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:28.008
The demo prints 1, 2

00:09:28.008 --> 00:09:30.275
3 forwards and 3, 2

00:09:30.275 --> 00:09:31.372
1 backwards.

00:09:31.372 --> 00:09:32.761
With the sentinel

00:09:32.761 --> 00:09:35.101
first.prev is the sentinel

00:09:35.174 --> 00:09:37.148
and first.prev.prev

00:09:37.221 --> 00:09:38.976
is the last node, 3.

00:09:38.976 --> 00:09:40.658
And on the empty list

00:09:40.731 --> 00:09:42.925
first returns null and forward

00:09:42.998 --> 00:09:44.460
is the empty string.

00:09:45.040 --> 00:09:46.823
To sum up the lecture.

00:09:46.823 --> 00:09:49.740
One: a list is an ordered sequence.

00:09:49.821 --> 00:09:52.089
An array shifts its elements

00:09:52.170 --> 00:09:53.872
on insert and delete

00:09:53.872 --> 00:09:56.140
a linked list relinks them

00:09:56.140 --> 00:09:59.057
and you must never break the chain.

00:09:59.057 --> 00:10:01.407
Two: a singly linked list is

00:10:01.488 --> 00:10:03.270
O(1) at the front

00:10:03.351 --> 00:10:06.916
and two or three references walking together

00:10:06.997 --> 00:10:10.400
find the middle or reverse it in one pass.

00:10:10.750 --> 00:10:13.826
Three: a doubly linked list adds prev

00:10:13.826 --> 00:10:16.659
so every local change is O(1)

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:19.168
a circular list links the last

00:10:19.249 --> 00:10:21.111
node back to the first

00:10:21.111 --> 00:10:24.510
and a sentinel removes the special cases.

00:10:24.860 --> 00:10:25.722
Try this.

00:10:25.818 --> 00:10:28.501
A bug like swapped links can

00:10:28.596 --> 00:10:31.183
make a list end in a cycle.

00:10:31.183 --> 00:10:32.812
Write hasCycle

00:10:32.812 --> 00:10:36.165
in O(n) time and O(1) space.

00:10:36.165 --> 00:10:39.422
Hint: the slow and fast pointers.

00:10:39.422 --> 00:10:41.434
If there is a cycle

00:10:41.434 --> 00:10:44.020
fast eventually laps slow.

00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:48.050
Next, lecture 8: applications of lists.

